es that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "**Initial Assessment: Rocket Time!**\n\nOkay, so the game's started, and things look...interesting. I'm dealt a decent hand, nothing spectacular, but definitely playable. I'm a Peasant, next to the Landlord who just threw down a 2-2-2-4 – a bold opening! My gut says, *This is my chance to strike!*\n\nI've got the Black Joker and the Red Joker, the almighty Rocket, and my mind is racing. I realize I have a straight (4 to Ace!), but it's only useful if I can take control of the game. If I pass, who knows what the Landlord will play? They've shown their hand a bit with that opening, suggesting they are aggressive or have little else. My teammate might have *something* to beat that opening, but it's unlikely; a bomb or Rocket, maybe, otherwise, *I* have to do this.\n\nIf I hold on to the Rocket, I'll be in trouble down the line with the *brittle* hand I have. I'm short on those crucial high single cards, like 2s. If I have to defend a single later, I'll have to break apart my straight to do so! By letting the Landlord maintain control, they'll just keep playing, forcing me to destroy the straight to stay in the game and, eventually, lose it.\n\nIf I play the Rocket now, I stop their momentum cold. I can immediately unleash my 11-card straight and pressure the Landlord to respond with a bomb, a strategic play that might pay dividends down the line. That's a huge gamble, but one I have to take. It clears the board, puts the pressure on, and gives me a huge advantage. \n\nSo it's decided! Rocket it is!\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has opened with a very strong hand, a triplet of 2s with a kicker (2-2-2-4). Since 2 is the highest rank, this can only be beaten by a Bomb or a Rocket. My hand contains a Rocket and a potential 11-card straight (4-5-6-7-8-9-10-J-Q-K-A). If I pass, I will likely be forced to break this straight to defend against single cards or pairs in future turns, as I lack any 2s to regain control. My hand is "brittle" and relies on seizing the initiative to play the long straight. Using the Rocket now stops the Landlord\'s high cards and allows me to play the straight immediately, putting the Landlord in a difficult position and leaving me with manageable remainders (two pairs).\n\nReason: I need to take control immediately to preserve my winning line (the 11-card straight). Allowing the Landlord to continue or forcing me to break my straight for defense would likely lead to a loss given my lack of other high control cards (2s).\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["BlackJoker", "RedJoker"]}'}
